    <item>
      <title>A 1,000-mile cloud on Mars may reveal physics never seen in nature</title>
      <author>
                    Tereza Ondráčková, wired.com
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/a-1000-mile-cloud-on-mars-may-reveal-physics-never-seen-in-nature/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/a-1000-mile-cloud-on-mars-may-reveal-physics-never-seen-in-nature/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">25d3d1fbc4a3a3d1f417b0cd9c0acc1484a2885b</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:50:28 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         A new model suggests Mars may host ice-forming physics never directly observed before.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/marscloud-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="Image of cloud on Mars" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/marscloud-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/marscloud-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/marscloud-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/marscloud-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The cloud always forms near the Arsia Mons volcano before extending into a long, wispy strand.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       ESA                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Every morning during the Martian spring and summer, a thin and extremely long cloud emerges, only to disappear again. The wispy &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/another-perfect-geometric-shape-detected-on-saturn/"&gt;formation appears&lt;/a&gt; near the volcano Arsia Mons and gradually elongates until it reaches over 1,000 miles—about the distance from New York to Kansas City as the crow flies—before dissipating. This cycle then repeats for several months.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Since it was first &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/a-probe-took-incredible-pictures-of-mars-on-its-way-to-a-far-off-asteroid/"&gt;recorded by a probe&lt;/a&gt; in 2018, scientists have been unable to find a plausible explanation for the existence of this cloud, often referred to as the Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud (AMEC). Until now. Recently, researchers &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/venus-may-have-devoured-its-moon/"&gt;successfully modeled&lt;/a&gt; the cloud’s formation for the first time, their results suggesting that it may be driven by a physical process that has never been directly observed in any planet’s atmosphere.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We found that we needed to include some exotic physics… physics that, while included in textbooks, is treated as theoretical and usually thought not to happen in nature. It certainly hasn’t been seen in action before,” Jorge Hernández-Bernal, a planetary scientist at Paris’ Sorbonne Université and lead author of the study, said in &lt;a href="https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Mars_s_oddest_cloud_may_be_even_odder_than_we_thought?ref=fomoera.com"&gt;a press release&lt;/a&gt;. “Once we included this physics in our simulations, the AMEC emerged just as we hoped.”&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;On Earth, clouds usually form when water vapor creates droplets or ice crystals in the presence of tiny particles, such as dust or salt. The new study, published in &lt;a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-026-02089-9"&gt;Nature Geoscience&lt;/a&gt;, instead suggests AMEC could be forming through so-called homogeneous nucleation, where vapor changes directly into ice crystals on its own without any help from debris.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“It’s akin to droplets of condensation appearing in the middle of a room, rather than on a window,” Hernández-Bernal explained. “It’s wholly unexpected.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This phenomenon requires extreme conditions—a sharp temperature drop of 30° Celsius within 10 minutes and high relative humidity. The Arsia Mons volcano also plays a key role. When wind current hits the &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/physics-predict-volcanic-eruptions/"&gt;65,000-foot-tall volcano&lt;/a&gt;, it creates a powerful wave that launches moist air high in the atmosphere in a matter of minutes.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Scientists have assumed a similar phenomenon could occur in the upper atmospheres of other planets, including Venus and even Earth, but it has &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/france-records-first-pyrocumulonimbus-cloud-wildfires/"&gt;never been observed&lt;/a&gt;. That’s not too surprising given the extreme air humidity required for this phenomenon, which is more than 100,000 times greater than any value measured on Earth.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Before this modeling, it was not known that these humidity levels existed &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/nasas-curiosity-rover-got-its-drill-stuck-on-a-rock-heres-how-they-freed-it/"&gt;on Mars&lt;/a&gt; either. “We don’t have nearly as much information about &lt;a href="https://www.wired.com/story/a-new-study-reveals-the-makeup-of-uranus-atmosphere/"&gt;Mars’ atmosphere&lt;/a&gt; as we do about Earth’s, so reproducing the AMEC to this degree is a big success,” said Hernández-Bernal, adding, “the result is remarkable.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This story originally appeared on&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.wired.cz/clanky/tajemny-mrak-na-marsu-merici-az-1-800-kilometru-odhalil-exoticke-fyzikalni-jevy" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" data-offer-url="https://www.wired.cz/clanky/tajemny-mrak-na-marsu-merici-az-1-800-kilometru-odhalil-exoticke-fyzikalni-jevy" data-event-click='{"pattern":"ExternalLink"}' data-event-boundary="click" data-in-view='{"pattern":"ExternalLink"}' data-include-experiments="true" data-reader-unique-id="46"&gt;WIRED Czech Republic and Slovakia&lt;/a&gt; &lt;em data-reader-unique-id="47"&gt;and has been translated from Czech.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
