    <item>
      <title>Hegseth warns prediction market bets warp military judgment, endanger troops</title>
      <author>
                    Jon Brodkin
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/pentagon-memo-on-prediction-markets-says-insider-bets-put-troops-lives-at-risk/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/pentagon-memo-on-prediction-markets-says-insider-bets-put-troops-lives-at-risk/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">f2a7a761b1a4bb318b3688a3ca8ba603fd544f60</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:28:43 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Pentagon memo: Insider trading gives US enemies key intel on military operations.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/polymarket-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="Screens displaying the logo and home page of prediction market Polymarket." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/polymarket-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/polymarket-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/polymarket-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/polymarket-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Getty Images | AFP/Martin Lelievre                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth issued a memo warning military personnel against using nonpublic information to make bets on prediction markets, saying the wagers could give important clues to enemy countries.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Do Not Compromise Military Operations for Personal Financial Gain,” said the subject line of an &lt;a href="https://assets1.cbsnewsstatic.com/hub/cms/prod_cms_alt/file/2026/10/22/58a4e0e3-ea5f-49e2-b7fe-3bb3381c5987/hegseth_memo__1_.jpg.pdf"&gt;October 20 memo&lt;/a&gt; published yesterday &lt;a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/news/hegseth-betting-ban-military-operations/"&gt;by CBS News&lt;/a&gt;. Hegseth’s memo was sent to senior Pentagon leadership, Combatant Command leaders, and Defense Department field activity directors.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Hegseth wrote:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Our enemies watch movements in these markets. When an American Service member, intelligence professional, or other Department civilian engages in a financial transaction on a military outcome, they create a signal. That signal can be aggregated, correlated, and exploited by hostile foreign intelligence services to anticipate our movements, targets, and timing. The lives of our Service members often depend on the enemy not knowing what is coming. The men and women who carry our flag into harm’s way deserve a Force that protects them—not one that exposes them to the enemy for personal gain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;In April, a US Army soldier was &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/04/soldier-won-410k-in-polymarket-bets-on-timing-of-maduro-capture-us-alleges/"&gt;arrested&lt;/a&gt; and charged with using confidential information to make $410,000 in Polymarket bets on the timing of the military’s capture of Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro. An August &lt;a href="https://acdatacollective.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/ACDC-Classifying-Insider-Trading-Risk.pdf"&gt;report&lt;/a&gt; by the Anti-Corruption Data Collective said the group found 152 suspicious wallets that placed longshot bets on military and defense markets and won a combined $8 million.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Betting can warp judgment, Hegseth says&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Hegseth’s memo said the prohibition applies to “all financial platforms,” including traditional betting services and prediction markets such as Polymarket and Kalshi. Defense Department personnel may not make any financial transaction informed by nonpublic information, regardless of whether that information is classified or unclassified, Hegseth wrote.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;“All Department of War personnel are prohibited from engaging in financial transactions using nonpublic information, either directly or indirectly through a third party… Violations may result in administrative, disciplinary, and, where appropriate, criminal action under the Uniform Code of Military Justice and/or applicable federal law,” Hegseth wrote.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The memo said betting on military matters related to a person’s job can warp one’s judgment. “Beyond operational security, financial stakes can warp judgment,” he wrote. “A logistician with money riding on a strike date has an interest in that date. An analyst with a position on an outcome has an interest in that outcome.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Kalshi and Polymarket both prohibit insider trading. But criminal charges of the sort described by Hegseth are sure to be a bigger deterrent than being banned from the Kalshi or Polymarket platforms.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;White House issued warning after suspicious bets&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;This was not the first warning issued internally by the Trump administration, The Associated Press &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/hegseth-prediction-markets-pentagon-3fc284334ba2c192c6c729ec592443f5"&gt;wrote&lt;/a&gt; yesterday.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“A group of new accounts on Polymarket made &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/polymarket-iran-trump-ceasefire-prediction-markets-350d9fe5ffefa74080ff5dd973aef48b"&gt;highly specific, well-timed bets&lt;/a&gt; on whether the US and Iran would reach a ceasefire just before President Donald Trump announced it in April, resulting in hundreds of thousands of dollars in &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/polymarket-kalshi-trump-iran-prediction-congress-d16d7bdf9a56cc1466b44baaf634aeeb"&gt;profits for the new customers&lt;/a&gt;,” the AP wrote. “The White House warned staff against using private information to trade on prediction markets the same day the report came out.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In another incident, a Trump teleprompter aide &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/07/trump-teleprompter-aide-made-100000-betting-on-what-trump-would-say-reports-say/"&gt;reportedly&lt;/a&gt; made $100,000 on Kalshi by betting on what Trump would say in prepared remarks.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/a-senator-tried-to-ban-gambling-on-prediction-markets-now-shes-a-kalshi-lobbyist/"&gt;Prediction markets have flourished&lt;/a&gt; under the Trump administration’s light-touch approach to regulation. The Trump administration is also trying to stop state governments from regulating the markets, arguing that state laws are preempted by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission’s exclusive jurisdiction. Courts have reached different conclusions, and state governments want the Supreme Court to uphold their right to impose restrictions on prediction-market gambling.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>pentagon</category>
      <category>prediction-markets</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Updated Google privacy policy says your Gmail might be used for advertising</title>
      <author>
                    Ryan Whitwam
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/updated-google-privacy-policy-says-your-gmail-might-be-used-for-advertising/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/updated-google-privacy-policy-says-your-gmail-might-be-used-for-advertising/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">32e337ef3d5208bf6923501146e0977f0f5f870e</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:19:55 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Turning on Personal Intelligence gives the company more data for ad targeting.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-640x360.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-1536x864.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-2048x1152.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-980x551.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/google-logo-big-g-grooves-1440x810.jpg 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Aurich Lawson                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Using Google’s products and services has always been fundamentally precarious—doing so gives the world’s largest advertising company a lot of juicy personal data. Google has long said it won’t use the sensitive data from products like Gmail, Drive, and Photos for ad personalization, but a change to the company’s privacy policy appears to dismantle that firewall.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;On October 16, Google issued an update to its &lt;a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"&gt;privacy policy&lt;/a&gt;, spotted by &lt;a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/google-personal-intelligence-privacy-3727111/"&gt;Android Authority&lt;/a&gt;. The ad personalization section used to include a clear limit on data from certain apps. “We don’t show you personalized ads based on your content from Drive, Gmail, or Photos,” the document said. Now, Google has updated it with the following language.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="ars-lightbox"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="585" height="287" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Google-ads-privacy.png" class="fullwidth full" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;               Updated language on when your sensitive data might inform ads.                               &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                    Credit:                                       Google                                   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This equivocation opens the door to using data from apps like Gmail and Photos in ads when it’s shared with other Google services, and it just so happens Google has a mechanism for doing that, with Personal Intelligence. The company released Personal Intelligence &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/01/gemini-can-now-scan-your-photos-email-and-more-to-provide-better-answers/"&gt;in early 2026&lt;/a&gt;, allowing users to hand data from multiple apps to Google AI products. The idea is that the added context from your existing data will lead to better AI answers, but it’s also proving to be a privacy minefield.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Personal Intelligence connects your data to the Gemini app but also to features like AI Mode search. While the Gemini app does not currently have ads, you may see sponsored content in AI search tools. Google’s changes to the privacy policy appear to acknowledge that data shared with AI search via Personal Intelligence could, therefore, contribute to ad personalization.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;According to Google, this clarification still doesn’t mean ads are “based directly” on your personal data. Here’s the company’s full statement:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Connecting your apps is entirely opt-in. Ads will continue to be relevant to things like your query, the context of the response and your interests. Ads will not be based directly on your Gmail inbox or Photos library, and as always, sensitive information will not be used to tailor ads.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Google’s statement is essentially a roundabout way of confirming that Workspace and Photos data may inform ads. If you interact with AI Mode while Personal Intelligence is enabled, there’s a chance that summaries and insights from your data will inform the answers you get. Because that same data is used for ad targeting, it’s accurate to say that your personal data is an element of ad targeting in AI search. The privacy policy now makes that clear.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;A Google spokesperson tells Ars that its privacy clarification does not indicate a change in how it handles your data with respect to its advertising partners. It’s not scanning your entire inbox to enhance your advertising profile, and it’s not giving any of this data to third parties. But if you pipe personal data into an AI search tool, the ads it shows you could be based on that information.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;The personalization trap&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The core of the problem is that Google wants its products and services to talk to each other. Google sees that wealth of data as a key strength of its AI efforts. Some apps like Gmail are still given protected status in Google’s data handling, but there are now more avenues for that data to get out. The more data you share across Google’s ecosystem, the greater the risk to your privacy.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We’ve seen a similar exception in the way Google &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/04/googles-privacy-maze-how-gemini-traps-you-and-your-data/"&gt;uses your AI interactions for model training&lt;/a&gt;. While Google says it doesn’t use personal data for AI training, it does use your inputs and the model outputs for that purpose. With Gemini integrated with so many services and connected to them via Personal Intelligence, those outputs may very well contain elements of your personal information. Google has said it tries to filter that content from the user activity controls, therefore keeping it out of training sets. However, it doesn’t promise it will always be successful.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Google is correct that Personal Intelligence is opt-in, but the company uses various prompts and pop-ups to entice users to turn it on without fully explaining the consequences. You may have turned it on without realizing.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If you want to ensure ads never use your personal data in this way, you’ll need to make sure Personal Intelligence is turned off. The easiest way is to access the Gemini settings for Personal Intelligence and disable the connected apps. These settings are also a few levels deep in your main Google account settings under Data &amp;amp; privacy &amp;gt; Personalization settings &amp;gt; Manage Personalized Recommendations in Search Services &amp;gt; Connected Content Apps. From there, you can disable connections to Workspace and Google Photos.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>advertising</category>
      <category>artificial-intelligence</category>
      <category>google-2</category>
      <category>privacy</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Inside the Pentagon, the pressure is on to find a home for a new spaceport</title>
      <author>
                    Stephen Clark
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/10/senior-commander-says-space-force-needs-new-spaceport-by-2030/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/10/senior-commander-says-space-force-needs-new-spaceport-by-2030/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">71db2933d3dcdb634a115e28fba72b3cb0ad7aa2</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:33:58 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Space Force considering options: public-private, federal installation, state land.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gallery-gallery_ng-2-ascent-2-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gallery-gallery_ng-2-ascent-2-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gallery-gallery_ng-2-ascent-2-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gallery-gallery_ng-2-ascent-2-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gallery-gallery_ng-2-ascent-2-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       Blue Origin's second New Glenn rocket lifts off from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station on November 13, 2025.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Blue Origin                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Leaders in the US Space Force would like to have a new spaceport up and running in four years, adding a third heavy-lift launch site to relieve pressure from bases in Florida and California that have shot rockets into orbit for nearly 70 years.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The obvious problem with that ambition is 2030 is four years away, and Pentagon officials have not determined where the new spaceport should go or how to acquire land for it.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Air Force Secretary Troy Meink first mentioned the service’s search for a third launch base in May. A congressionally mandated study of the Space Force’s launch infrastructure identified the need for “another site that’s capable of heavy and super heavy launch capability,” Meink said in a House Armed Services Committee hearing.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Trump administration &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/08/trump-admin-calls-for-more-spaceports-to-handle-surge-in-launches/"&gt;released a space transportation policy&lt;/a&gt; in August that endorsed the construction of more launch and reentry sites for commercial and military missions. This week, the Space Force general responsible for procuring launch services for the military laid out an “aspirational” timeline for a new launch site.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We need this capability by 2030, but I’m also a pragmatist,” said Lt. Gen. Philip Garrant, head of Space Systems Command. “That is not an inconsequential demand to build a spaceport when you factor in orbital regimes, safety, access to commodities, the Americans that live near it. In many ways, it’s much like an airport, but even louder. So there’s a lot of consideration.”&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;h2&gt;Public, private, or both?&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;But there’s no firm plan yet for when the military might get moving on actually setting up a new launch base. “It’s going to take a strong partnership with local and state governments,” Garrant said. “As far as decision timelines, we don’t have anything yet.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There were 175 orbital launch attempts last year from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station and Kennedy Space Center in Florida and Vandenberg Space Force Base in California. Space Force officials project the country’s established spaceports will need to support 700 launches per year by 2036.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;A third launch site would offload some of the launch demand from limited real estate at Cape Canaveral and Vandenberg. It would also provide what Space Force leaders call “geographic resiliency” to mitigate the risk of a natural disaster or military attack knocking the Cape or Vandenberg offline.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The federal government already owns a third spaceport at NASA’s Wallops Flight Facility in Virginia, used for suborbital rocket tests and small- and medium-class satellite launches. Small rockets can also launch from Pacific Spaceport Complex, owned by the state of Alaska.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There are also commercial options that might be available for a new military spaceport. Ars asked Garrant if the Space Force might consider leasing property from a privately owned launch base, such as the expansive spaceport &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/08/spacex-intends-to-invest-up-to-100-billion-in-massive-louisiana-spaceport/"&gt;SpaceX plans to build in southern Louisiana&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We’ve discussed that we need to consider all of those opportunities: public-private partnership, new federal installation, state land, leveraging commercial land—all of those are possibilities. They all have pros and cons. We haven’t made that decision yet, but we’re not discounting any of those options.”&lt;/p&gt;</description>
