    <item>
      <title>Crows can evaluate visual info twice as fast as mammals</title>
      <author>
                    Jacek Krywko
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/crows-can-evaluate-visual-info-twice-as-fast-as-mammals/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/crows-can-evaluate-visual-info-twice-as-fast-as-mammals/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">f4ff97932acae9eda58d0ea683683cd2f8f05c31</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:32:58 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Birds&amp;#8217; compact nervous system seems to help them handle tasks very quickly.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1472974929-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="Image of a black crow looking quizzically at the camera." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1472974929-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1472974929-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1472974929-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1472974929-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/close-portrait-of-a-carrion-crow-royalty-free-image/1472974929?phrase=carrion%20crow&amp;amp;searchscope=image,film"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Santiago Urquijo                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="" name="page-1" data-page="1" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/crows-can-evaluate-visual-info-twice-as-fast-as-mammals/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;Much of what we know about how a brain makes decisions comes from studying monkeys watching dots. In a task known as a random-dot-motion task, a cloud of flickering specks drifts weakly in one direction while the subject tries to determine whether—and in which direction— it’s moving. The task has shown that in the primate neocortex, neurons gather visual evidence over time, with their firing rates climbing until they cross a threshold, when the animal commits to a decision.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But avian brains don’t have a neocortex, so it wasn’t obvious how they made similar decisions.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“This was the big question,” said Andreas Nieder, a neurobiologist at the University of Tübingen. “How [would] a differently organized avian brain encode decisions?”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;To find out, Nieder and his colleague Philipp Schmidbauer put two carrion crows through the same task the monkeys faced, recording their brain activity along the way. The crows solved the task the same way primates do, using the same mathematical logic despite having a very different brain structure. And they did it two times faster.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;A brain built different&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Nieder and Schmidbauer put two hand-raised crows in front of a touch-sensitive monitor showing a cloud of white dots on black, with some fraction of them drifting coherently left or right or up or down. The birds’ job was to report the overall direction by pecking one of the targets that flanked the display. Correct answers earned them a mealworm.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We intentionally wanted to have exactly the same random-dot motion task,” Nieder said. “The only difference was that the crows had to peck to indicate their decision, whereas monkeys were indicating by making an eye movement.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The researchers varied how many dots moved together, ranging from 81 percent of the dots coherently moving together, which is trivially easy to determine, down to 3 percent, which is barely distinguishable from noise. They also included a zero percent condition, in which there was no correct answer.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;Both birds scored better than chance, even when only three percent of the dots were moving.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;While the crows worked at the task, the team recorded from 248 neurons in the NCL (nidopallium caudolaterale)—a region at the top of the avian processing hierarchy that is widely regarded as the functional counterpart of the primate prefrontal cortex, despite being built differently.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Birds and mammals parted evolutionary ways more than 360 million years ago, and bird brains evolved a different architecture. The mammalian cortex is arranged as stacked sheets of cells, while the equivalent tissue in a bird is organized into dense clusters, with no layering at all. This alternative architecture, Nieder’s study found, proved faster.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Measured from the moment that the dots appeared to the moment the bird’s head started turning to peck at the button, the crows averaged 235 milliseconds. In closely matched versions of the task, monkeys took roughly 410, 490, and 600 milliseconds in three studies. The obvious question was whether the birds simply sacrificed accuracy for speed, but it turned out they did not.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="record-pageview" name="page-2" data-page="2" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/crows-can-evaluate-visual-info-twice-as-fast-as-mammals/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Hunting for shortcuts&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;“This would be a simple explanation, that they are very fast at the cost of precision. But that’s not what’s happening,” Nieder said. Crows were as good as monkeys or humans in the same task.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We’ve never forced the crows to be as quick as possible,” Nieder explained. “We just gave them a self-paced decision. And even in this situation, they were twice as fast compared to any monkey or human that I’m aware of.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;One possible reason for the crows’ speed was that their’ brains were working on a fixed deadline, and the data initially seemed to support that. In primates, difficulty has a large effect on how long a decision takes. In the crows, reaction times ran from 220 milliseconds at the easiest task to just 254 at the hardest, a difference of only 34 milliseconds. But Nieder doesn’t think this means an avian brain is just wired to produce answers on a timer.&lt;/p&gt;                                                      &lt;p&gt;“If you zoom in, there is a statistical increase in the reaction time when the coherence is going down,” Nieder said. “They show the same effect as mammals. It’s just much less pronounced, because everything happens much faster.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There are no shortcuts, he explained, as the crows had to look at the direction of dots’ movement and process this information—they didn’t have any other cues. “That’s why I think they do accumulate evidence. They have to,” Nieder said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Catching the evidence&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The recordings of neuronal activity show the evidence accumulation in action. Just under a quarter of the neurons sampled, 58 of 248, fired differently depending on which direction the bird would eventually choose, and these neurons started registering that decision incredibly early—about 70 milliseconds after the dots appeared and roughly 170 milliseconds before the bird’s head moved. “The neurons were accumulating sensory evidence, which is very reminiscent of what monkey neurons would do in the neocortex,” Nieder said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This kind of signal in the brain is called a decision variable, a running tally of evidence that can climb toward a commitment. “It turns out that what we find in the crow brain looks basically the same,” Nieder said. “And that’s interesting considering their brains have evolved very distinctly, as if evolution has found the same physiological mechanism twice, independently.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;So far, the team can’t attribute the crows’ speed to any one particularly fast process. Nieder’s leading explanation is that nearly every biological process runs faster in birds than in mammals.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Life in the fast lane&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We cannot really point our finger to one specific process that is totally faster. It is probably a cascade of different processes that are faster,” Nieder explained. This cascade, he said, begins with vision, since avian visual systems are exquisitely movement-sensitive. It continues through the decision itself and extends to the motor response. Nieder argues there are likely two physiological aspects that make this possible.&lt;/p&gt;                                                      &lt;p&gt;The first is simply size. Flight imposes strict weight limits, so birds pack enormous numbers of very small neurons into very small brains. Their axons, the long cable-like fibers that transmit electrical and chemical signals between cells, are shorter.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Long lines take time. Short lines speed everything up,” Nieder said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The second factor is temperature. Humans’ body temperature is about 36.6° Celsius, while birds go up to 41, or even 42°. This higher body temperature speeds up all chemical processes in birds’ bodies.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Every ion channel would open and close faster. Every transmitter would move through the synaptic cleft faster,” Nieder explained. “Evolution really took their bodies to the limit. This is life in the fast lane, not only in the brain, but also respiration, the cardiovascular system—everything is going toward the limit in birds.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But calling crows (or birds in general) decision-making speed demons comes with a few caveats.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Picking the smartest&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;First, the study included only two birds, a rather small sample. “On the one hand, that’s a valid constraint,” Nieder said. “On the other hand, when you look at monkey studies, that’s the standard number of animals that we have for these very complex types of studies, combining behavior and electrophysiology.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Also, the decision-making time of 235 milliseconds in crows and above 400 milliseconds in primates is not an absolute limit. These times apply to the random dot-motion task, which inherently requires waiting to see which way things are drifting.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Finally, it might be hard to learn whether the results extend beyond corvids or crows specifically. “The problem with many other birds is that they are not as smart as corvids and crows and you need a smart bird in order to train it to do exactly this task,” Nieder said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;So far, he admitted, we can’t be sure whether excellent decision-making skills of crows in his lab are due to birds’ brains being speedy in general or to crows simply being smarter than other avians. “Among birds, the corvids are renowned to be extremely intelligent, so maybe there’s something special about them,” Nieder said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Science, 2026.  DOI: &lt;a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aed6459"&gt;10.1126/science.aed6459&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>biology</category>
      <category>birds</category>
      <category>brains</category>
      <category>neurobiology</category>
      <category>visual-processing</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel gets waiver after worries fuzzy US ban on foreign-made robots may hit fabs</title>
      <author>
                    Jeremy Hsu
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/intel-gets-waiver-after-worries-fuzzy-us-ban-on-foreign-made-robots-may-hit-fabs/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/intel-gets-waiver-after-worries-fuzzy-us-ban-on-foreign-made-robots-may-hit-fabs/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">fb0c8f10981348f0d84c26a3d32deaf34d6f9ee8</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:18:31 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Company says foreign-made robotic vehicles are crucial to US chip production.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Intel-fab-interior-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="The interior of Intel&amp;#039;s chip manufacturing facility named Fab 52 in Chandler, Arizona." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Intel-fab-interior-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Intel-fab-interior-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Intel-fab-interior-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Intel-fab-interior-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The interior of Intel's chip manufacturing facility named Fab 52 in Chandler, Arizona.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/newsroom/press-hub/press-kit/client-computing/press-kit-intel-technology-tour-2025.html"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Intel                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;US chip champion Intel was so confused by the US government ban on foreign-made robots that it requested and received a waiver “out of an abundance of caution.” The tech company wanted to make sure that it could continue using certain robotic vehicles in its US chip fabs.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The uncertainty surrounding the Federal Communications Commission ban on certain foreign-made robots was revealed in the agency’s decision to grant Intel’s waiver request. On October 22, the &lt;a href="https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/DA-26-1146A1.pdf"&gt;FCC published its order&lt;/a&gt; allowing Intel to continue using the foreign-made robots in US semiconductor manufacturing facilities for a period of one year.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The robots in question are &lt;a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/learn/types-of-robots.html"&gt;automated guided vehicles&lt;/a&gt; operating on “tracks or predefined paths” while often requiring operator oversight, according to Intel’s website. They help to move materials and items around within warehouses and factory floors.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Such robotic vehicles are typically manufactured by Japanese companies like Murata Machinery and Daifuku Co., according to &lt;a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-19/intel-asks-fcc-for-upgrades-to-banned-foreign-robot-technology"&gt;Bloomberg News&lt;/a&gt;. But the exact types of robotic vehicles used by Intel were only identified in a “confidential appendix” that accompanied its request.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The FCC’s ban mainly covers new models of mobile robots, like &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/08/world-humanoid-robot-games-show-runners-breaking-records-bursting-into-flames/"&gt;humanoid robots&lt;/a&gt; and robot vacuums, and still allows for the import of robots previously approved for sale in the United States. But the &lt;a href="https://www.fcc.gov/document/fcc-adds-foreign-produced-power-inverters-and-robots-covered-list-0"&gt;agency rules&lt;/a&gt; seem to restrict major modifications to “previously authorized devices.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Intel’s waiver request stated that it was “unclear” whether its robotic vehicles even qualify as robots covered by the FCC ban but said it sought relief just in case. The tech company also received approval to “make certain hardware, software, and firmware changes” that would help ensure the continued operation and maintenance of the robotic vehicles.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;The tech company described the robotic vehicles as being “critical to its domestic semiconductor manufacturing operations” and argued that the ability to maintain the robots was crucial to avoiding any disruptions to chip production. It also said there are no US-made alternatives that meet the company’s requirements.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The FCC approval came within a week of when Intel filed its waiver request on October 16. That is not too surprising given the importance of Intel to the Trump administration’s plans for reshoring chip manufacturing—not to mention that the US government took an &lt;a href="https://www.intc.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/1748/intel-and-trump-administration-reach-historic-agreement-to"&gt;$8.9 billion equity stake&lt;/a&gt; in the chip company.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meanwhile, the US robotics industry at large is still grappling with the &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/07/who-wins-and-who-loses-after-us-bans-foreign-robots/"&gt;consequences of the FCC ban on foreign-made robots&lt;/a&gt;, given that China is the world’s leading supplier of robots and robotic components. Ars previously talked with one &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/08/us-distributor-of-chinas-most-popular-humanoid-robots-pivots-after-us-ban/"&gt;US robotics distributor that has pivoted&lt;/a&gt; from importing &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/09/founders-cost-cutting-obsession-drove-unitree-lead-in-cheap-humanoid-robots/"&gt;China’s humanoid robots&lt;/a&gt; to opening a US manufacturing facility for robots.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Some analysts have warned that the US government’s attempt to boost domestic production of robots via the restrictions on foreign-made robots could backfire for US industry. A report by the market intelligence firm &lt;a href="https://www.idc.com/resource-center/blog/us-robotics-ban-humanoids-europe/"&gt;International Data Corporation&lt;/a&gt; looks forward to 2030 and estimates that the FCC rule could reduce US revenue from deploying humanoid robots by 58 percent compared to pre-restriction expectations.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
